Subject: Re: possible pkgsrc emacs framework improvements?
To: Thomas Klausner <wiz@NetBSD.org>

(DRAFT -- not sent.)

Hi Thomas,

The rest of the sweep finished, so here are the numbers I owed you.
Of the 57 packages I built -- everything that reads modules.mk except
ten that want bazel, TeX Live, rust or a gcc bootstrap, none of which
has anything to do with where the lisp goes -- 36 build and 21 fail.
Thirteen fail because of this change, two fail behind one of those
thirteen, and six for reasons of their own.  This is on a tree with
none of my fixes applied; measuring while fixing, which is what I did
the first time round, reported four fewer.

The thirteen fall into four shapes, and each takes one line.

Four get a 404 fetching their distfile, because mk/fetch/github.mk has
GITHUB_PROJECT?= ${PKGBASE} and the prefix moves PKGBASE, so the fetch
asks for github.com/ziglang/emacs30-zig-mode and WRKSRC points
where the archive does not unpack: zig-mode, lua-mode, emacs-neotree
and markdown-mode.

Two name a renamed package without the prefix and can then never be
satisfied: misc/emacs-wiki asks for semi>=1.14.6, and devel/flim adds
an unprefixed apel>=10.8 to BUILDLINK_API_DEPENDS.apel while apel's
own buildlink3.mk already says ${EMACS_PKGNAME_PREFIX}apel>=10.  There
is a third that no build here can reach: devel/doxymacs asks for
w3-[0-9]* inside .elif ${EMACS_VERSION_MAJOR} <= 21, and www/w3
becomes emacs20-w3-4.0b47nb4, so the line can never be satisfied --
but only under emacs20 or emacs21, which is why the sweep went
straight past it.  Those three are all there are on trunk; I matched
on the name boundary rather than as a substring, which first gave me
xemacs-packages for emacs-packages and w3m for w3.

Three have a PLIST that spells share/emacs/site-lisp itself while the
install follows ${EMACS_LISPPREFIX}, so the files go to the version
directory and the file check reports them missing from where the PLIST
says: twittering-mode, emacs-dict-client and rsltc.

Five spell ${PKGBASE} on one side of the install and the bare name on
the other, which agree only while the prefix is empty.  Three are in
the lisp directory: editors/matlab-mode creates
${EMACS_LISPPREFIX}/${PKGBASE:C|^xemacs-||} and installs into
${EMACS_LISPPREFIX}/${PKGBASE}, textproc/xslide installs into
${EMACS_LISPPREFIX}/${PKGBASE} while its PLIST says
${EMACS_LISPPREFIX}/xslide, and www/emacs-w3m sets WRKSRC to
${WRKDIR}/${PKGBASE}-${GITHUB_TAG}.  Two are in share/doc:
editors/gnuserv lists share/doc/${PKGBASE} in INSTALLATION_DIRS while
doc-install writes share/doc/gnuserv, so that name becomes a file
holding whichever README was installed last rather than a directory,
and textproc/dictem has the same pair.

There is a fifth shape that does not fail at all, and it is the one I
could not have found by reading.  devel/cqual and
inputmethod/tamago-tsunagi build green and install into
share/emacs/site-lisp rather than into the version directory: their
PLIST and their install both spell the old path, so the two agree and
nothing catches it.  cqual does not spell EMACS_PKGNAME_PREFIX either,
so it stays cqual-0.991nb21 and writes exactly where it always did.  A
package that quietly ignores the new location seems worse to me than
one that stops, and only the packing list of the built package shows
it:

  cqual-0.991nb21                        share/emacs/site-lisp/cqual/
  emacs30-tamago-tsunagi-5.0.7.1nb3      share/emacs/site-lisp/egg/
  the other 35 that built                share/emacs/30.2/site-lisp/

The other six are not about the change: three have lost their distfile
(cobol-mode 402, flyspell and ispell-emacs 404), mail/mu has a PLIST
bug of its own, mail/vm reaches misc/bbdb2 -- which you have since
removed, and vm no longer asks for it -- and mail/wl-snapshot is
stopped by a CONFLICTS line that I think is stale.  The last three
come back further down.

The two that fail behind another are devel/semi and www/emacs-w3m,
both waiting on devel/flim.  emacs-w3m is worth a word because it
shows how soft this kind of count is: with a flim available it gets as
far as its own ${PKGBASE} problem and fails there instead, so whether
it lands in the thirteen or in the two depends only on what was
installed when I ran it.  Its own bug does not go away either way.

The sweep tree is a 2 September copy, so I went back and checked each
package I name above against trunk today rather than trusting the
copy: all four GITHUB_PROJECT lines are still absent, all three
unprefixed dependencies are still unprefixed, all six hand-written
lisp paths are still hand-written, and all five ${PKGBASE} pairs are
still as described.

Four of these are already visible in pkgsrc today, because xemacs
already has a prefix.  I built an xemacs-nox11 21.5.36 here with your
modules.mk and the packages untouched, so this part is measured
against the tree as it is rather than against my branch:

  editors/gnuserv    share/doc/xemacs-gnuserv is made while doc-install
                     writes share/doc/gnuserv, so that name becomes a
                     484-byte file holding whichever README went last
  textproc/dictem    installs share/doc/xemacs-dictem/* while the PLIST
                     asks for share/doc/dictem/*
  editors/matlab-mode
  textproc/xslide    stop earlier, at the byte-compile:
                       matlab-mode  Cannot open load file: "tempo"
                       xslide       Cannot open load file: "sendmail"
                     Neither library is in XEmacs; both are in
                     editors/xemacs-packages and neither package depends
                     on it.  devel/doxymacs and textproc/migemo-elisp
                     already carry that dependency in their xemacs
                     branch, so the line has a precedent to copy.  With
                     it added they reach the ${PKGBASE} problem after
                     all, and with both fixes they build:
                     xemacs-matlab-mode-2.3.1nb4, xemacs-xslide-0.2.2nb4.

So this is not a new class of breakage the change introduces.  It is a
class that exists now, that only xemacs users meet, and that the change
makes everyone meet.  I think that argues for it rather than against.

Some of what I found does not depend on the change at all, and I have
put it here rather than in a mail of its own, because the packages
overlap so much with the list above that two mails would name most of
them twice.

devel/ecb cannot be built by anything.  Adding GITHUB_PROJECT= ecb gets
it past the fetch, and it then stops at

  Symbol's function definition is void: semantic-mode

which ecb-2.50's own Makefile writes into the compile script.  With
xemacs-nox11-21.5.36nb1 and xemacs-packages-1.19nb1 installed,
(fboundp 'semantic-mode) is nil bare and after (require 'semantic), and
the package takes xemacs215, xemacs215nox, xemacs214 and xemacs214nox
and nothing else.  editors/xemacs-packages already installs ECB, 63
files under lib/xemacs/xemacs-packages/lisp/ecb, so XEmacs users have
one either way.  Whether devel/ecb should follow ECB upstream, depend
on the bundled one, or be marked is not mine to decide, so there is no
diff for it.

misc/elscreen cannot be built either, and this one is recent.  Its
EMACS_VERSIONS_ACCEPTED is emacs20 and it includes apel's buildlink3.mk
at top level; apel takes emacs29 and up and names emacs20 in
EMACS_VERSIONS_INCOMPATIBLE.  The dependency is real -- elscreen.el
requires alist and static, both apel -- so there is no version where
the two can meet.  I read this at first as fallout from 12 September
because both files carry that date, but a 2 September copy already has
apel at 1.45 with the same list, so the overlap went on 26 August and
the September commit only removed emacs21 from a list that had already
stopped working.  Its own comment says to try wip/elscreen-current.  No
diff: whether it should follow wip or be marked is a maintainer's call.

devel/semi does not fail to build with its semi-bbdb option set -- it
fails to parse, because options.mk includes misc/bbdb2/buildlink3.mk
and bbdb2 went out with emacs21.  Pointing it at bbdb3 was my first
try and it is wrong: mime-bbdb.el is BBDB 2 code and byte-compiles with
bbdb-record-getprop, bbdb-record-putprop, bbdb-search-simple,
bbdb-pop-up-bbdb-buffer, bbdb-record-edit-notes and bbdb-annotate-notes
all undefined, and none of the six is in bbdb-3.2.2a under any name.
The diff takes the option out instead, with the reasoning where the
block was.  What is missing for the feature is a port of mime-bbdb.el,
not a line in options.mk.

mail/mu cannot be packaged against an Emacs without D-Bus -- every nox
one, and an emacs30 with the dbus option off:

  pkg_create: can't stat .../site-lisp/mu4e/mu4e-dbus.elc

mu4e/meson.build asks the Emacs whether dbusbind is compiled in and
only byte-compiles mu4e-dbus.el when it is, while PLIST.emacs lists the
.elc unconditionally.  The guard is not needed: both requires in the
file pass 'noerror, and against emacs30-nox11 it byte-compiles with no
output and the .elc loads.  Nobody builds mu against a nox Emacs today,
which is why nobody has seen it.

Then a handful of declarations that no longer describe anything.
editors/gnuserv's CONFLICTS names xemacs-[0-9]* and
xemacs-current-[0-9]*, and neither does what it looks like: the
packages are xemacs-21.4.25, xemacs-nox11-21.4.25, xemacs-21.5.36 and
xemacs-nox11-21.5.36, so the second matches nothing and the first
misses the nox ones.  I found it by having it happen -- pkg_add refused
xemacs-nox11 over bin/gnuattach with gnuserv installed, which is the
collision the line exists to declare.  Four more match nothing at all
and cost nothing today, but the thing they were guarding may still be
true: devel/pcl-cvs (emacs-21.*), inputmethod/skk (ddskk-[0-9]*),
mail/mew (mew-xemacs-[0-9]*) and inputmethod/tamago-tsunagi
(tamago-anthy-[0-9]*, only in wip).  I checked each with pkg_admin
pmatch rather than by eye, which was worth doing: mail/elm's looked
wrong and is fine, because its PKGNAME is elm-2.5.8nb5 and not the
elm2.5.8 its DISTNAME suggests.

One goes the other way -- it matches, and I do not think it should.
misc/bbdb3 has CONFLICTS+= xemacs-packages-[0-9]*, and it stopped a
build here: mail/wl-snapshot wants bbdb and pkg_add refused the bbdb3
package because xemacs-packages was installed.  bbdb3 takes emacs29,
emacs30 and emacs31 and no xemacs at all, so it cannot produce a file
under lib/xemacs; I compared the two packing lists rather than
reasoning about it, and of bbdb3's 38 files and xemacs-packages' 7840
not one name is shared.  There is no diff for it because I would
rather ask: is that line left from when bbdb3 accepted xemacs, or is
there a collision I am not seeing?

Two of the things that had to be fixed were in my own modules.mk, and
only building found them.  EMACSLOADPATH for the build still named the
old shared site-lisp, so anything byte-compiling against another elisp
package stopped with "Cannot open load file" -- devel/flim could not
require apel's install.el.  Nothing in the checks I had showed this:
EMACS_LISPPREFIX was right, and the installed Emacs finds install.elc
with the same flags the build uses.  Only the environment variable
pointed at the old place.  Separately, the EMACS_VERSION_REQD path
still had the empty-_EMACS_PKGDIR fatal I thought I had fixed, because
I had only fixed it on the EMACS_VERSIONS_ACCEPTED side.

A third was in something I had already told you was done.  I wrote
that the choice reads the package's list in the order it was written,
the way pyversion.mk walks PYTHON_VERSIONS_ACCEPTED, and that was the
intent but not the code: the loop that intersects the two lists walked
modules.mk's, so the package's order never reached the answer.
misc/mailcrypt writes emacs20 first and still got emacs31.  The loop
runs the other way now.  Reordering does move the answer, but only
where it should -- with EMACS_TYPE set to something the package does
not take, mailcrypt given "emacs20 emacs29" picks emacs20 and given
"emacs29 emacs20" picks emacs29, while if the default is in the list
the default still wins.  I mention the condition because I first tried
to show this with devel/apel and got the same answer both ways: apel
names emacs20 in EMACS_VERSIONS_INCOMPATIBLE, so only one candidate
survived the filter and there was nothing left to order.

The pbulk part was worse, and I am sorry to have said it was done.  I
wrote that it was three lines in mk/pbulk/pbulk-index.mk of the same
shape as the python and ruby entries.  It was three lines and they did
nothing: the class also has to be named in _PBULK_MULTI, which is the
list the loop walks, so nothing ever read them.  It also wanted the
filtered list rather than the written one, for the reason python takes
_PYTHON_VERSIONS_ACCEPTED -- otherwise a package naming a version
pkgsrc no longer has puts it in the bulk index.  So it is four lines,
and with them devel/apel gives

  PKGNAME=emacs30-apel-10.8.0.20201107nb2
  PKGNAME=emacs29-apel-10.8.0.20201107nb2
  PKGNAME=emacs31-apel-10.8.0.20201107nb2

with the default first, while devel/pcl-cvs, which takes emacs20 and
nothing else, gives the single line it should, and devel/gmake, which
never includes modules.mk, is untouched.

I should say where all this was measured, since it is one machine for
most of it: NetBSD 11.0/amd64.  The parts that matter most I have also
run on i386 11.0, in a throwaway VM -- apel, flim, zig-mode, dictem,
gnuserv and emacs-w3m build there with the prefix on their names, the
pbulk enumeration comes out the same, and Emacs finds the lisp under
share/emacs/30.2/site-lisp with share/emacs/site-lisp still beside it.
Nothing else has been near another platform.

mail/mu turned out not to be about placement at all -- its lisp goes
exactly where it should, and what is wrong with it is further down.
It is worth one word here though, because it is the kind of thing your
third point would find on its first pass: nobody builds mu against a
nox Emacs today, so nobody has seen it.

It also settled a question I had about the prefix, though not the way I
first read it.  emacs30 and emacs30-nox11 conflict with each other and
share share/emacs/30.2/site-lisp, so one emacs30-foo could serve
either.  I had written that mail/mu said otherwise, because its file
list differs between an Emacs with D-Bus and one without -- and then
noticed that the difference is made by the very guard the mu4e patch
below removes.  With that gone I went looking for any other
package whose installed files depend on the Emacs having X, and there
is none: nothing in the 87 conditions on it, in a PLIST or anywhere
else.  So the two builds do not need two names, and I have folded them:

  EMACS_PKGNAME_PREFIX=	${_EMACS_TYPE:C/nox$//}-

A package accepting emacs30 now accepts emacs30nox as well, so the
lists stop having to spell both halves of every pair, and the bulk
index makes one package per version instead of two -- devel/apel goes
from six entries to three, textproc/xslide from eleven to six.  The
dependency is written so either build satisfies it, and pkg_add is
what enforces it: with only emacs30-nox11 installed here, the same
binary package refuses or installs according to that one line.

  @pkgdep emacs30>=30.1<31              pkg_add: no pkg found, sorry.
  @pkgdep {emacs30-nox11,emacs30}>=...  installs

The exception keeps the longer name.  A package that refuses the X
build says so in EMACS_VERSIONS_INCOMPATIBLE, and is then called
emacs30-nox11-foo with its dependency pinned to that build, so a
binary package cannot land on an Emacs that cannot run it.  A package
that refuses the nox build keeps the plain name, since there is no
other emacs30-foo to confuse it with.  cad/dinotrace-mode is the only
candidate I found -- it carries a comment about needing athena widgets
-- and I have not touched it, because its list also still names
emacs26 and I have not built it either way.

XEmacs stays split by series rather than by build: xemacs214- and
xemacs215-, which today are both xemacs-, although 21.4 and 21.5 are
different enough that editors/xemacs and editors/xemacs-current are
separate packages.

One failure is the change refusing, which is what you asked for.
mail/vm in my copy depends on misc/bbdb2, which accepts only emacs21,
and the build stops with

  ERROR: No Emacs that pkgsrc still has is accepted here
  ERROR: Accepted versions are: emacs21 emacs21nox
  ERROR: pkgsrc has: emacs31 emacs31nox ... xemacs214nox

rather than with "Cannot open /version.mk".  It cannot happen on trunk,
though: you took bbdb2 out with emacs21 and mail/vm rev 1.47 no longer
includes its buildlink3.mk.  The same goes for the fifteen packages I
said still ask for emacs21 -- that is my 2 September copy, not pkgsrc;
jde, leim21, bbdb2 and nxml-mode went with the version and nothing on
trunk names it.  So the intersection is worth taking for the next
retirement rather than for a mess sitting there today.

Since my earlier figures came from that copy, I took them again from
trunk, fetching each Makefile and PLIST.  The build results above are
still from the copy, with the current modules.mk dropped in; every
package I name still exists on trunk with the same lines.  87 packages
read modules.mk, leaving out wip and the emacs and xemacs packages
themselves.  86 have a PLIST -- counting PLIST.emacs and the like, not
just PLIST, which is where I lost one the first time.  Of those, 78
write ${EMACS_LISPPREFIX}, six write the path by hand (the same six I
named before) and two install no lisp at all.  Of the 26 that pass a lisp directory into their own
build, all 26 pass the variable.  (I said 25 and named chat/zenicb last
time; that was a miscount -- what I matched there is a commented-out
PLIST_SUBST line, and zenicb passes nothing into a build at all, it
writes ${EMACS_LISPPREFIX} straight into INSTALLATION_DIRS and its
install rule.)

Three of those six need a second line, because the install writes the
shared path as well and changing only the PLIST splits the two:
inputmethod/tamago-tsunagi and devel/cqual, which leave it to a
bundled makefile, and devel/rsltc, whose own Makefile has
${DESTDIR}${PREFIX}/share/emacs/site-lisp/rsl spelled out three times.
All six build here with both lines.  Left alone, tamago-tsunagi and
cqual are the two that build green into the old directory, and the
other four stop -- which side you land on depends only on whether the
PLIST or the install was the one written by hand.

devel/rsltc carries a caveat: it wants devel/gentle, which pkgsrc
marks broken on this platform, so the only reason it got as far as the
packing list here is that I had a gentle installed from the experiment
below.  The mismatch itself is in the files and does not depend on
that.

rsltc took a detour worth mentioning on its own, and it ends somewhere
I did not expect.  It wants devel/gentle, which carries
BROKEN_ON_PLATFORM= ${LP64PLATFORMS}, so I had written it off.  With
that line taken out for the experiment, on NetBSD 11.0/amd64, gentle
builds with nothing but K&R-era warnings, and it really runs: the
distfile ships ast.g and no ast.c, and the log has gentle turning all
eleven grammars into C.  rsltc builds against it and works:

  $ rsltc HELLO.rsl
  Checking HELLO ...
  rsltc completed: 0 error(s) 0 warning(s)
  $ rsltc bad.rsl
  bad.rsl:3:13: syntax error

with the line and column right.  The same thing was then tried from
the same tree on i386, where pkgsrc does not mark gentle broken at
all, and there it builds, installs, and dies on the first grammar:

  gentle ast.g
  gmake: *** [std_make:31: ast.c] Segmentation fault (core dumped)

That first i386 run was on a throwaway NetBSD 9.4 VM, which differs
from my amd64 box in release as well as in word size, so I ran it
again on i386 11.0 and got the same thing, on the same first grammar.
Three points now:

  amd64 11.0   gentle builds, all eleven grammars, rsltc works
  i386  9.4    gentle builds and installs, segfault on ast.g
  i386  11.0   the same

so it is the word size and not the release.  What the line says is the
other way round: it calls LP64 broken, and LP64 is where it worked.  I
have no core to offer -- both i386 runs were throwaway VMs -- and I am
not asking you to change the line on three data points and no
backtrace.  But since i386 is not marked broken, devel/rsltc is
failing there in any bulk build today, and that is also what keeps it
out of the one on amd64.

The name is the expensive half, and most of it is outside modules.mk.
EMACS_PKGNAME_PREFIX only does anything where the package spells it in
PKGNAME, and 24 of the 87 do not -- 22 last time; I had been reading
only the Makefile and missing the ones that set PKGNAME in options.mk
or not at all.  Adding it moves PKGBASE, and six
variables under mk/ take ${PKGBASE} as their default -- PKGWILDCARD
and PKG_SYSCONFVAR in bsd.pkg.mk, FILEBASE in
pkgformat/pkg/package.mk, and GITHUB_PROJECT, GITLAB_PROJECT and
CODEBERG_PROJECT in mk/fetch -- so anything resting on the default
moves with it.  Ten of the 87 fetch from GitHub without setting
GITHUB_PROJECT; none uses gitlab or codeberg.  Nine spell ${PKGBASE}
themselves -- the nine above.

None of that shows up until a package gains the prefix, so I took one
of the 22 that lack it -- misc/color-theme -- added the PKGNAME line
and built it.  It breaks twice over: MASTER_SITES is
.../releases/${PKGBASE}/, which becomes
.../releases/emacs30-color-theme/ and 404s, and the install writes
ELISPDIR=${EMACS_LISPPREFIX}/${PKGBASE} while the PLIST says
${EMACS_LISPPREFIX}/color-theme:

  ERROR: .../site-lisp/emacs30-color-theme/themes/color-theme.elc

That is one of the 24.  Three of those 24 spell ${PKGBASE}, and they
are the ones to look at first: misc/color-theme above,
mail/mailutils (--with-mh-bindir=${PREFIX}/libexec/${PKGBASE} and
DOCDIR=share/doc/${PKGBASE}) and www/emacs-w3m
(WRKSRC=${WRKDIR}/${PKGBASE}-${GITHUB_TAG}).  The other 21 have no
${PKGBASE} of their own, so for them it may well be the one line --
but I would rather not say so until they have been through a build.

One more, which I found late and have since fixed, but it is worth
showing because it was mine and because of where the answer was.
Making EMACS_TYPE the default rather than a requirement means a
package that does not accept it falls back to one it does accept.  On
its own that is pyversion.mk's behaviour and fine; in a chain it was
not.  www/emacs-w3m accepts emacs20 and devel/apel does not, so with
EMACS_TYPE=emacs20 emacs-w3m asked for emacs20-apel while apel built
itself as emacs29-apel, and nothing said why the dependency never
appeared.

pyversion.mk does not have that problem, and the reason is one I had
walked past: when pkgsrc resolves a dependency it passes the pattern
as PKGNAME_REQD, and pyversion.mk reads the version back out of it, so
the dependency is built for the version its dependent chose.  With the
same six lines here:

  => Full dependency emacs20-apel>=10: NOT found
  => Verifying reinstall for ../../devel/apel
  ERROR: This package does not build with emacs20
  ERROR: Accepted versions are: emacs29 emacs29nox ... emacs31nox

which names the version, the package and what would work.  It is where
the tree stops today too, though with "No valid Emacs version
installed found" and no version named.

While I was in those files I tidied one thing that caused the trouble
in the first place.  Each nox version.mk is a copy of its X11 twin with
one line changed, and the copied lines are where the rot was; each
buildlink3.mk then writes the version a third time in
BUILDLINK_ABI_DEPENDS, on the line after the one that takes it from
version.mk.  With the nox file including its twin and the ABI floor
moved into version.mk, the version is written in exactly one place per
Emacs and cannot disagree with itself:

  editors/emacs29/version.mk      3 lines carry a version
  editors/emacs29/buildlink3.mk   0

Making EMACS_TYPE a request rather than the answer turns one idiom
from harmless into wrong, and there is exactly one package using it.
devel/apel has

  .if empty(EMACS_TYPE:Mxemacs)
  .  if !empty(EMACS_TYPE:Memacs2[3-9]*) || !empty(EMACS_TYPE:Memacs3[0-9]*)
  PLIST.emacs23=	yes
  .  else
  PLIST.emacs19=	yes

which reads what the user asked for.  Today that is always what gets
built, so it is correct by accident.  With the fallback it is not:

  EMACS_TYPE=emacs20     built for emacs29, PLIST.emacs19=yes
  EMACS_TYPE=xemacs215   built for emacs29, PLIST.emacs19=yes

apel does not accept emacs20 or xemacs, so it is built for emacs29 and
then given the Emacs 19 packing list.  EMACS_VERSION_MAJOR is the
version actually in use and six other packages already use it; with
the test written that way all four inputs give emacs23 and it builds.
It is the only package in the 87 that tests EMACS_TYPE in a
condition, so this is one line, not a sweep.

I owe you a correction on the first mail.  I wrote that nothing in
pkgsrc names an Emacs the tree no longer has, having checked emacs21.
That was true of emacs21 and not of everything: cad/dinotrace-mode has

  EMACS_VERSIONS_ACCEPTED=	emacs26 emacs29

and editors/emacs26 is gone.  It costs nothing today because emacs29 is
still in the list, which is exactly why it has sat there -- a value
nobody reads because nothing reads it.  The diff takes emacs26 out.
The comment above it worried me, since it says the package wants athena
widgets and I did not want to narrow it on a guess, so I built it: with
the line as emacs29 alone it builds against emacs29-nox11, which has no
toolkit at all, and the lisp loads.  I have left the comment, because
it records why the list is short and I have not tried emacs30 or 31.

That build also showed something the file check cannot: the lisp lands
in share/emacs/29.4/site-lisp/emacs29-dinotrace-mode, the prefix inside
the directory name, because the install spells ${EMACS_LISPPREFIX}/
${PKGBASE}.  Its PLIST spells ${PKGBASE} too, so the two agree and
nothing complains -- green, and named wrong.  It is the same shape as
cqual and tamago-tsunagi being green in the old shared directory.

One more that is not about placement at all.
EMACS_VERSIONS_INCOMPATIBLE is set by devel/apel, and no line of
modules.mk reads it, while pyversion.mk and rubyversion.mk both
subtract their equivalent from what they will choose from.  Now that
the selection walks the package's own list, leaving it out is one more
condition in the same loop, so I have done that.  Side by side, with
EMACS_TYPE=emacs30nox and devel/apel:

  modules.mk as it is  apel-10.8.0.20201107nb2, and the same again
                       when told EMACS_VERSIONS_INCOMPATIBLE=emacs30nox
  with the line read   emacs30-apel        by default
                       emacs29-apel        told emacs30nox is no good
                       emacs30-nox11-apel  told emacs30 is no good

The last line is the whole of the nox handling in one place: refusing
the X build is the one thing that puts nox back into a package name.

apel's own line names emacs20, which it does not accept anyway, so
nothing moves there -- that line has been doing nothing twice over,
which is presumably why nobody noticed the variable was never read.

I had been saying the lisp goes somewhere Emacs already searches
without ever checking it, so I did.  I installed the ones that build,
listed every .elc they own -- 390 files -- and asked Emacs for each by
the name it would be required under.  All 390 are on load-path:
share/emacs/30.2/site-lisp and its first-level subdirectories, with
share/emacs/site-lisp still there beside them, so a package that has
not moved yet is unaffected.  Requiring the main library of each
package works for 24 of 33.  The nine that fail are worth a word,
because I nearly wrote them up as damage: they fail for reasons inside
the elisp, not for where it lives.  markdown-mode and rmail-mime want
eieio-make-class-predicate, which this Emacs does not have even after
(require 'eieio); verilog-mode wants XEmacs's lucid; gnuserv wants
XEmacs's temp-directory.  I copied three of the nine into the old
shared site-lisp and required them from there, and they fail with the
same messages, character for character.

The lisp moves and the info files do not, and that turns out to matter
more than it sounds.  _EMACS_INFODIR is picked by flavour, not by
version, so every GNU Emacs gets ${PKGINFODIR} and every XEmacs gets
lib/xemacs/site-packages/info.  With the version in the name, two
builds of the same package are two packages that write the same info
file, and pkg_add stops the second:

  pkg_add: emacs29-skk-17.1nb2: conflicting PLIST with
           emacs30-skk-17.1nb2: info/skk.info

That is inputmethod/skk built twice, once against each Emacs, and 30
of the 87 install info files.  Getting as far as being able to run
that took three other things first, and they are worth listing because
each was invisible while only one Emacs could be installed:
editors/emacs29-nox11/version.mk still says 29.1 although the package
it builds is 29.4, so the lisp went to share/emacs/29.1/site-lisp and
the build's load path pointed at a directory that does not exist;
EMACS_BIN is ${PREFIX}/bin/${_EMACS_FLAVOR}, so building for emacs29
byte-compiled with whichever Emacs owned bin/emacs, which was 30.2;
and the two Emacs packages themselves will not coexist.  I first hit it by
accident, rebuilding flim against the stock modules.mk while the
prefixed one was still installed, which is the same collision wearing
different names.  The two apels sat side by side happily through
all of it, because apel installs no info file and flim installs two.
XEmacs has the same shape: xemacs214 and xemacs215 both give
lib/xemacs/site-packages/info.

Three other variables are picked the same way and I have taken two of
them with the lisp.  EMACS_ETCPREFIX was share, so emacs29-mew and
emacs30-mew would both write share/mew; it is now
share/emacs/<version>/etc, which is safe because the two packages that
use it have the path baked in at build time and nothing has to be told
where it went.  math/ess spells ${EMACS_ETCPREFIX} on both sides and
needs nothing; mail/mew passes it to the build but writes share/mew in
its PLIST, so it wants the one-line change the three lisp ones wanted,
and the diff is below.  EMACS_BIN was
${PREFIX}/bin/${_EMACS_FLAVOR}, so building for emacs29 byte-compiled
with whichever Emacs owned bin/emacs; it is now the versioned binary
for GNU Emacs.  XEmacs keeps the plain name, and that is not an
oversight I have left lying: 21.5 installs bin/xemacs-21.5-b36 but
21.4 installs no versioned executable at all, only a
bin/xemacs-21.4.25-<hash>.dmp, so there is nothing to point at.  I
built 21.4 here to find that out rather than guess a name.

The third is EMACS_INFOPREFIX, and that one I have left alone on
purpose.  Info is found at run time, not baked in:
Info-default-directory-list comes from the Emacs's own --infodir and
the only runtime lever is INFOPATH.  Moving the elisp packages' info
under the version would put it somewhere Emacs does not look.  It also
would not be the first collision there -- 66 of the 93 files the two
Emacs packages share are their own info files -- so it is the same
change as making the Emacsen coexist rather than a separate one.

So being able to build every version is not yet being able to install
them.  Moving info under the version as well is the obvious answer and
I have not done it, because Emacs finds info through INFOPATH and the
dir file rather than by looking beside the lisp, so it is a change
with a second half I would rather agree with you first.

Then I went one level up and found the same thing, larger.  I was
building skk for emacs29 to show the collision above, and the Emacs
itself would not install:

  pkg_add: emacs29-nox11-29.4: conflicting PLIST with
           emacs30-nox11-30.2nb1: bin/ctags

editors/emacs29 and editors/emacs30 do not declare a conflict with
each other -- each names only its own nox twin, and the built packages
carry @pkgcfl emacs29-[0-9]* and @pkgcfl emacs30-[0-9]* and nothing
else -- but their file lists overlap in 93 places: bin/emacs,
bin/ctags, bin/etags, bin/emacsclient, bin/ebrowse, include/
emacs-module.h, 66 info files and 15 under share.  So two versions of
Emacs cannot be installed side by side today, and nothing says so
until pkg_add trips over it.  python313 versions every one of its
binaries -- bin/python3.13, bin/pydoc3.13 -- which is why py312- and
py313- packages are worth having on one machine.

I am not proposing to fix that here; it is a bigger change than the
three you asked about, and it is yours to want or not.  But it does
set the ceiling on what the second point buys: with the Emacsen as
they are, a per-version name is worth having because a bulk build can
make every version and because the name records which Emacs a binary
package was made for -- not because two of them can sit on one machine
yet.  The info collision above is the same disease one floor down.

One cost of the second point that I have not seen raised.  Putting the
prefix in PKGNAME takes the package out of the vulnerability list's
reach, quietly:

  pkg_admin audit-pkg flim-1.14.2             -> reports CAN-2004-0422
  pkg_admin audit-pkg emacs30-flim-1.14.2     -> nothing
  pkg_admin audit-pkg xemacs-flim-1.14.2      -> nothing

The third line is the one that matters: this is already true of the
xemacs- packages in pkgsrc today.  An advisory can be written to cover
it, but only by enumerating -- {,emacs30-}flim<1.14.3 matches while
*-flim<1.14.3 and emacs*-flim<1.14.3 do not, because the glob does not
combine with a version bound.  That shape is at least not a novelty:
of the 30234 entries in the list 3778 already open with a brace list,
1183 of them with the empty alternative, and w3m{,-img}<0.3.2.2 shows
a brace and a version bound together.  So the cost is the width of the
line rather than a new mechanism -- with the prefix carrying the
flavour only it is {,xemacs-}, and with the version in it every
advisory for an elisp package has to name every Emacs.  Nothing in the
current list is affected: I decompressed it and went through the 92
entries whose name starts with emacs or xemacs, and every one is
bounded below what pkgsrc ships.  The only advisory for an elisp
package rather than for Emacs itself is flim<1.14.3, and pkgsrc has
1.14.9.

CONFLICTS goes the same way, and that one has teeth.
inputmethod/tamago-tsunagi says CONFLICTS= tamago-[0-9]*, which matches
tamago-20020909nb5 and does not match emacs20-tamago-20020909nb5:

  pkg_admin pmatch 'tamago-[0-9]*' tamago-20020909nb5          matches
  pkg_admin pmatch 'tamago-[0-9]*' emacs20-tamago-20020909nb5  does not

Two packages that both select emacs20 would then land in the same
share/emacs/20.7/site-lisp/egg with nothing declared between them.
Spelling the prefix in the pattern fixes it and sharpens it at the
same time -- with ${EMACS_PKGNAME_PREFIX}tamago-[0-9]* the line matches
emacs20-tamago while tamago-tsunagi is built for emacs20, and does not
match it under emacs30, which is right: the two only collide when
they share a lisp directory.  That one is in emacs-framework/pkg-fixes/
on the branch, with the rest of the package changes; say the word and I
will paste whichever of them you want to look at into a mail instead.

I watched the shape of this happen for real, though for a different
reason: editors/gnuserv's CONFLICTS names a pattern that matches no
package, and pkg_add refused xemacs-nox11 over bin/gnuattach instead
of the line refusing it first.  So wherever the prefix moves PKGNAME,
the CONFLICTS lines pointing at that package want looking at.  The
vulnerability list is the same mechanism without a fix I can offer, so
I would rather put that half in front of you than decide it.

The package diffs are below.  The framework itself -- modules.mk,
pbulk-index.mk, and the one-line changes to the Emacs packages' own
version.mk and buildlink3.mk that go with it -- is on the branch
rather than pasted here, since that part is what we are talking about
rather than something to apply.  They go on to trunk as
of today in the order given and reversed, with no offset and no fuzz.

Happy to split any of this up however suits you -- the package fixes
stand on their own whether or not you want the framework part.

Showta

--- devel/apel/Makefile.orig
+++ devel/apel/Makefile
@@ -40,8 +40,13 @@
 .include "../../editors/emacs/modules.mk"
 
 PLIST_VARS+=	emacs23 emacs19
-.if empty(EMACS_TYPE:Mxemacs)
-.  if !empty(EMACS_TYPE:Memacs2[3-9]*) || !empty(EMACS_TYPE:Memacs3[0-9]*)
+# Ask which Emacs is being built for, not which one the user asked for.
+# They are the same today because EMACS_TYPE is used as the answer, but
+# it is a request, and a package that does not accept it is built for
+# something else -- and then this test reads the request and picks the
+# wrong list.  EMACS_VERSION_MAJOR is the version actually in use.
+.if ${EMACS_FLAVOR} != "xemacs"
+.  if ${EMACS_VERSION_MAJOR} >= 23
 PLIST.emacs23=	yes
 .  else
 PLIST.emacs19=	yes

